\begin_body

\begin_layout Itemize
Recall that all valid quantum states of a system comprise that system's Hilbert space
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a single qubit,
 any state in its Hilbert space can be written as a quantum superposition of computational basis states,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{0},\ket{1}\right\} $
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi}=c_{0}\ket{0}+c_{1}\ket{1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The state is said to belong to the Hilbert space of the quantum system
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Recall:
 we can represent 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}$
\end_inset

 as a vector of expansion coefficients (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

amplitudes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) over the computational basis states,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ \ket{0},\ket{1}\right\} $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{c}
\text{coefficient for }\ket{0}\leftrightarrow\\
\text{coefficient for }\ket{1}\leftrightarrow
\end{array}\left[\begin{array}{c}
c_{0}\\
c_{1}
\end{array}\right]=c_{0}\left[\begin{array}{c}
1\\
0
\end{array}\right]+c_{1}\left[\begin{array}{c}
0\\
1
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Composite Hilbert Spaces and Composite States
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When we have two qubits,
 we have two Hilbert spaces,
 say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset

,
 for qubits 0 and 1
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We might label a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}_{0}$
\end_inset

 of qubit 0 with a subscript 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

0
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 indicating it is within 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{0}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Similarly,
 we might label a state 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}_{1}$
\end_inset

 of qubit 1 with a subscript 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

1
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 indicating it is within 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}_{1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We form a two-qubit state using a 
\series bold
tensor product
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\ket{\psi}_{1}\otimes\ket{\psi}_{0}=\ket{\psi}_{0}\otimes\ket{\psi}_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The tensor product commutes,
 but we will prefer the form 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi}_{1}\otimes\ket{\psi}_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 since it keeps the bits numbered in order from most significant to least significant,
 as we go from left to right (just like when we're writing bit strings)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Tensor products are also known as Kronecker products
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can use more compact notation,
 where we leave the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\otimes$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 implicit:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\ket{\psi}_{1}\otimes\ket{\psi}_{0}=\ket{\psi}_{1}\ket{\psi}_{0}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
