Exercise 5.3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Let a two-qubit state be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=c_{0}\ket{0_{2}}+c_{2}\ket{2_{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Determine whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\Psi}$
\end_inset

 is a product state.
 If so,
 find the states 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{B}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\ket{\psi_{A}}$
\end_inset

 for qubits 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

#+html:
 <details><summary>Show/hide solution...</summary>It can be helpful to write this as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=c_{0}\ket{0_{B}}\ket{0_{A}}+c_{2}\ket{1_{B}}\ket{0_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

It is clear that we can factor this as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\Psi}=\left(c_{0}\ket{0_{B}}+c_{2}\ket{1_{B}}\right)\ket{0_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

Now,
 we can write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ket{\psi_{B}}=c_{0}\ket{0_{B}}+c_{2}\ket{1_{B}}\qquad\text{and}\qquad\ket{\psi_{A}}=\ket{0_{A}}
\]

\end_inset

#+html:
 </details>
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{B}\left(\alpha\right)$
\end_inset

 of measuring 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\left\{ 0,1\right\} $
\end_inset

 on qubit 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Newline newline
